            <ArticleTitle>[Twenty-four hour blood pressure rhythm in patients with hypertension and ischemic heart disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>50-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To elucidate relationship between presence of ischemic heart disease and 24-hour blood pressure (BP) profile.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty four hour BP index characterizing degree of nocturnal blood pressure fall was measured in patients with hypertension without coronary heart disease (n=65, group 1), with hypertension and effort angina (n=35, group 2), with effort angina and normal BP (n=25, group 3).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Values of 24-hour BP systolic and diastolic index were in group 1 12.7+/-1.3 and 16.0+/-1.1%, respectively, in group 2 5.8+/-2.4 and 7.4+/-3.6%, respectively, in group 3, 6.0+/-2.2 and 6.0+/-3.0%, respectively (p&lt;0.05, groups 2 and 3 vs group 1).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Decreased nocturnal BP lowering could be a compensatory reaction aimed at maintenance of adequate blood flow through stenosed coronary vessels.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
